::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L05 GP Q01::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Perspective: Source checks help students choose stronger research evidence. Which slide note is clearest? {
~A. Stuff about sources and research#Incorrect. This wording is vague and would not help the audience understand the evidence.
~B. Sources can be on websites and in other places.#Incorrect. This is broad and does not explain the source-checking strategy.
=C. Check author, date, and purpose before using a source.#Correct. This slide note is concise, clear, and connected to the presentation perspective.
~D. Research is something students do in school.#Incorrect. This is too general and does not support the specific perspective.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L05 GP Q02::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.RS.01
Which evidence source is most credible for a digital presentation about student research habits? {
~A. A random anonymous comment online#Incorrect. Anonymous comments are difficult to verify and are weak evidence for an academic presentation.
~B. A friend's guess about research habits#Incorrect. A guess is not reliable enough to support a digital presentation.
=C. A school survey showing how students choose online sources#Correct. A school survey is relevant and provides evidence connected to the presentation topic.
~D. A joke about students searching the internet#Incorrect. A joke is not reliable research evidence.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.04 U06 L05 GP Q03::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Weak slide note: The research source thing is helpful because people can see whether source stuff is good. Which revision is clearest? {
=A. Source checks help students judge whether evidence is reliable.#Correct. This revision is concise, clear, and uses precise language.
~B. People can look at the thing and know stuff.#Incorrect. This wording is still unclear and informal.
~C. Source stuff is good for many things.#Incorrect. This remains vague and does not improve clarity.
~D. Research is research when people research it.#Incorrect. This is circular and does not explain the idea.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L05 GP Q04::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.04
Which phrase uses academic vocabulary appropriately for a slide about evidence? {
~A. pretty good source stuff#Incorrect. This phrase is informal and vague.
=B. credible evidence from a reliable source#Correct. This phrase uses precise academic vocabulary appropriate for the topic.
~C. random facts from somewhere#Incorrect. This phrase suggests weak evidence and lacks precision.
~D. things that help maybe#Incorrect. This wording is too uncertain and unclear.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L05 GP Q05::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
A slide has a full paragraph of evidence copied onto it. What is the best revision choice? {
~A. Add another full paragraph to balance the slide.#Incorrect. More text would make the slide harder to read.
~B. Remove all evidence from the presentation.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed; it should be presented clearly.
~C. Make the paragraph smaller so more words fit.#Incorrect. Smaller text does not solve the clarity problem.
=D. Use a concise evidence note on the slide and explain the evidence orally.#Correct. This keeps the slide clear while preserving the support.
}
